  "summary": "The world's finest hats are woven from straw so fine it reads as linen — and they are Ecuadorian.",
  "description": "The Panama hat is woven in Ecuador from plaited toquilla straw (Carludovica palmata), its grades measured in weaves per square inch — a superfino or \"golden fleece\" can take six months of finger-work and hold water like ceramic. The name stuck because Panama-bound gold-rush travelers and Theodore Roosevelt's canal visit wore them, but the tradition belongs to Pile, Montecristi and Jipijapa weavers whose hatter workshops bleach, block and finish each crown. UNESCO inscribed the weaving in 2012; weavers' hands, thumbs and fingernails shaped by decades of plaiting are part of the documented craft lore.",
  "culturalContext": "The Montecristi hat is Ecuador's craft ambassador mislabeled by canal-era commerce: Pile and Jipijapa weavers' superfino grades (six months of finger-work) anchor a UNESCO-listed (2012) tradition, and the hat's story — of Ecuadorian mastery under a Panamanian name — is a national identity argument in straw.",
